loopback interface(lo)

In a Kubernetes pod, the loopback interface (lo) is a virtual network interface inside the pod's shared network namespace that routes traffic back to the pod itself via 127.0.0.1 (localhost). It allows different containers running inside the same pod to communicate directly with one another over the local network stack without sending packets outside the pod.


How It Works Under the Hood

• Shared Network Namespace. When Kubernetes creates a pod, it initializes a single Linux network namespace (often managed by a foundational pause container) and brings up the lo interface inside it. Every container scheduled into that pod joins this exact same network namespace rather than receiving an isolated one.

• Inter-Container Communication. Because all containers in the pod share the lo interface, Container A can reach a service running in Container B simply by calling localhost:<port> or 127.0.0.1:<port>. 
This setup mirrors how multiple processes talk to each other on a single traditional virtual machine.

• Bypassing External Interfaces. Traffic sent over lo stays entirely within the kernel's software network stack and never touches the pod's external virtual ethernet interface (eth0) or the cluster network.

