Key Use Cases & Binding Behavior

• Sidecar Patterns. Multi-container pods heavily rely on lo for helper containers. For example, a main web application can push logs or metrics to a sidecar collector over 127.0.0.1, or route outbound requests through a local service mesh proxy like Istio.

• Security & Interface Binding. An application inside a pod can choose which network interface it listens on to control visibility:
'''
	• Binding to 127.0.0.1 (lo). Restricts access strictly to processes and containers inside the same pod. This is ideal for internal admin dashboards, local caches, or helper daemons that external pods should never reach. '''

	• Binding to Pod IP (eth0). Exposes the service to traffic coming from other pods and services across the cluster.

	• Binding to 0.0.0.0. Listens on both lo and eth0 simultaneously, allowing both local localhost calls and external cluster traffic.


Troubleshooting & Pitfalls

• Port Collisions. ![alt text](image.png)Because containers in the same pod share one lo interface and IP space, two containers in the same pod cannot bind to the same port number (for example, two containers both trying to listen on port 8080).

• External Connection Refused. If a pod is running and healthy, yet other pods receive "Connection Refused" errors when calling its Pod IP, the application inside is likely bound only to 127.0.0.1 (lo) instead of 0.0.0.0 or eth0.


Pod-to-Pod networking
With Kubernetes, every node has a designated CIDR range of IPs for Pods. This ensures that every Pod receives a unique IP address that other Pods in the cluster can see. When a new Pod is created, the IP addresses never overlap. Unlike container-to-container networking, Pod-to-Pod communication happens using real IPs, whether you deploy the Pod on the same node or a different node in the cluster.


All containers inside a Kubernetes pod share a single, isolated Linux network namespace, meaning they share one IP address, one port space, and communicate directly with each other over localhost. In the Kubernetes Networking Model, this architecture allows a multi-container pod to behave much like a single virtual machine or physical host running multiple local processes.

Core Architectural Components

Several underlying Linux and container primitives work together to build and maintain the network inside a pod:
• Shared Network Namespace. When Kubernetes creates a pod, the container runtime sets up a dedicated Linux network namespace for it. Every container scheduled into that pod joins this exact same namespace rather than getting its own isolated network stack.
• The pause Container. Before your application containers start, Kubernetes launches a lightweight infrastructure container called the pause container. Its sole job is to hold the shared network namespace open and retain the pod's IP address even if an application container crashes and restarts.
• Virtual Ethernet (veth) Pair. To connect the pod's private network namespace to the host node's root network namespace, the Container Network Interface (CNI) plugin creates a veth pair. One end of this virtual pipe sits inside the pod (usually named eth0), while the other end attaches to the host node's network bridge or routing table.

How Intra-Pod Communication Works

Because containers in the same pod inhabit the same network environment, traffic between them follows standard local networking rules:
• Loopback Interface (localhost). Containers within the same pod reach one another via the lo (loopback) interface at 127.0.0.1 (localhost). For example, a main web server container can send logs to a fluentd sidecar container simply by sending traffic to localhost:24224.
• Shared Port Space. Because all containers share a single network interface and IP address, two containers in the same pod cannot bind to the same TCP or UDP port. Attempting to run two separate NGINX containers listening on port 80 inside one pod will cause a port conflict, though two different pods can both use port 80 without issue.
• Shared iptables and Routing. Any firewall rules, iptables chains, or routing tables configured inside the pod's namespace apply equally to all containers inside that pod. Service meshes like Istio rely on this behavior to inject an Envoy proxy sidecar that intercepts all incoming and outgoing traffic for the entire pod.

Exception: Host Networking (hostNetwork: true)

• Bypassing Pod Isolation. You can configure a pod with hostNetwork: true in its specification, which prevents Kubernetes from creating a separate network namespace. Instead, the pod's containers share the underlying worker node's network interfaces and IP address directly, which is typically reserved for system-level daemons like CNI agents or node monitoring tools.